      "text": "To solve for \\(\\tan V\\) in triangle \\(VWX\\) where \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\) and \\(VW = 3\\), we will use the definitions of the trigonometric functions in a right triangle. Specifically, we will use the tangent function, which is defined as the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side of an angle in a right triangle.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(VX\\) is the hypotenuse of the right triangle \\(VWX\\).\n- \\(VW\\) is one of the legs of the right triangle \\(VWX\\).\n- We need to find \\(\\tan V\\), which is \\(\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}\\).\n\nIn triangle \\(VWX\\):\n- The hypotenuse \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\).\n- The leg \\(VW = 3\\).\n\nThe side adjacent to angle \\(V\\) is \\(WX\\). Using the Pythagorean theorem, we can find \\(WX\\):\n\n\\[\nVX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the known values:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\n13 = 9 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(WX^2\\):\n\n\\[\nWX^2 = 13 - 9\n\\]\n\\[\nWX^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nTake the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nWX = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find \\(\\tan V\\):\n\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
