      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given a right triangle \\( VWX \\) with \\( VX = \\sqrt{13} \\) and \\( VW = 3 \\) , let's use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of \\( WX \\).\n\nFirst, recall the Pythagorean theorem for a right triangle:\n\\[\nVX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nPlugging in the given values:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying both sides:\n\\[\n13 = 9 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 9 from both sides:\n\\[\n4 = WX^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nWX = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find \\(\\tan V\\). In a right triangle, \\(\\tan\\) of an angle is the ratio of the length of the side opposite the angle to the length of the side adjacent to the angle.\n\nFor \\(\\angle V\\):\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
